import Sql from '../../components/explainers/querying/Sql.astro';
import LockFigure from '../../components/relational-next/a18/LockFigure.astro';
import worksheetUrl from '../../components/relational-next/a18/rehearse.py?url&no-inline';
const sections=[
 ['identify-the-decision-that-needs-protection','Identify the decision that needs protection'],
 ['cover-every-change-that-could-invalidate-the-decision','Cover every change that could invalidate the decision'],
 ['give-the-invariant-a-stable-coordination-row','Give the invariant a stable coordination row'],
 ['choose-the-lock-and-keep-it-through-the-decision','Choose the lock and keep it through the decision'],
 ['acquire-several-resources-in-a-consistent-order','Acquire several resources in a consistent order'],
 ['decide-what-waiting-and-skipping-mean-for-the-application','Decide what waiting and skipping mean for the application'],
 ['check-the-protocol-with-two-sessions-and-an-all-writers-argument','Check the protocol with two sessions and an all-writers argument'],
];
const schema=`CREATE TABLE resources (
  resource_id integer PRIMARY KEY,
  capacity integer NOT NULL CHECK (capacity >= 0)
);
CREATE TABLE reservations (
  request_id text PRIMARY KEY,
  resource_id integer NOT NULL REFERENCES resources,
  owner_id integer NOT NULL,
  UNIQUE (resource_id, owner_id)
);
INSERT INTO resources VALUES (21, 1), (22, 1);`;
const reserve=`BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL READ COMMITTED;

-- Command 1: acquire the coordination row. Require exactly one row.
SELECT resource_id
FROM resources
WHERE resource_id = 21
FOR UPDATE;
-- If no row: ROLLBACK and report the resource unavailable. Stop here.

-- Command 2: a fresh Read Committed snapshot, after acquisition.
INSERT INTO reservations (request_id, resource_id, owner_id)
SELECT 'R1', p.resource_id, 7
FROM resources AS p
WHERE p.resource_id = 21
  AND (SELECT count(*) FROM reservations AS r
       WHERE r.resource_id = p.resource_id) < p.capacity
RETURNING request_id;

COMMIT;`;
const combined=`-- A already holds 21 and has inserted R1, without committing.
-- B starts this command, then waits for A:
SELECT resource_id,
       (SELECT count(*) FROM reservations WHERE resource_id = 21)
FROM resources
WHERE resource_id = 21
FOR UPDATE;
-- A commits. In the recorded run B returned: 21 | 0.
-- A NEW command in B then counted 1.`;
const move=`BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL READ COMMITTED;
-- Both move directions acquire the same distinct IDs, one command each.
SELECT resource_id FROM resources WHERE resource_id = 21 FOR UPDATE;
SELECT resource_id FROM resources WHERE resource_id = 22 FOR UPDATE;
-- Require both classes; otherwise roll back.

SELECT resource_id, owner_id
FROM reservations WHERE request_id = 'R1' FOR UPDATE;
-- Require the expected source 21 and owner 7; otherwise roll back
-- and reconsider the operation with the reservation's actual location.

UPDATE reservations
SET resource_id = 22
WHERE request_id = 'R1' AND resource_id = 21 AND owner_id = 7
  AND (SELECT count(*) FROM reservations WHERE resource_id = 22)
      < (SELECT capacity FROM resources WHERE resource_id = 22)
RETURNING request_id;
-- Zero rows: move declined; R1 remains in 21. A uniqueness error:
-- failed transaction; roll back the whole attempt.
COMMIT;`;
const inspect=`SELECT p.resource_id, p.capacity, count(r.request_id) AS reserved,
       count(r.request_id) <= p.capacity AS within_capacity
FROM resources AS p
LEFT JOIN reservations AS r USING (resource_id)
GROUP BY p.resource_id, p.capacity
ORDER BY p.resource_id;`;
const reset=`-- Outside either transaction; only in this disposable exercise database.
TRUNCATE reservations, resources;
INSERT INTO resources VALUES (21, 1), (22, 1);`;
---

<p>A row lock lets a transaction keep a competing change waiting until its own decision is finished. The difficult part is choosing the row. Locking the records you happened to read does not necessarily protect the fact you relied on.</p>
<p>We will keep the class reservations from <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/">Making concurrent changes correct</a>, but remove its remaining-place counter. Free places now come from counting reservations. That saves a stored value that could drift, and gives us a decision spanning several rows: does this class have room for one more?</p>
<p>The protocol below uses <strong>PostgreSQL 18 at Read Committed</strong>. Its selected concurrent checks ran on native PostgreSQL 18.6. The figures are small illustrative schedules; the downloadable worksheet runs real sessions. The <a href="/review/relational-next/isolation/">isolation article</a> owns the broader comparison of snapshots and serialisable execution.</p>

<nav aria-label="In this article"><ol>{sections.map(([id,title])=><li><a href={`#${id}`}>{title}</a></li>)}</ol></nav>

<h2 id="identify-the-decision-that-needs-protection">Identify the decision that needs protection</h2>
<p>Class 21 has capacity one and no reservations. Mina and Tariq each ask for a place. Either may get it; both must not. Our <strong>invariant</strong>, the rule every committed state must satisfy, is that a class’s reservation count never exceeds its capacity. Each reservation also has a unique request identity, a real class and at most one booking per owner in that class.</p>
<p>There is no remaining counter in this version. The resource row holds capacity; the reservation rows record ownership.</p>
<Sql code={schema} label="The class is a resource; each accepted booking has its own row" />
<p>The keys and reference reject a duplicate request, duplicate owner or nonexistent class. They do not limit how many different owners can reserve a real class. A PostgreSQL <code>CHECK</code> on one row cannot enforce this count over other rows. We could return to the parent’s guarded counter design; here we are deliberately constructing the alternative that counts ownership directly.</p>
<p>Suppose both transactions count zero before either inserts. Both conclude that one more will fit. They insert different requests for different owners, so neither uniqueness constraint rejects them.</p>
<p>Play Figure 1 with <em>Reservation rows</em>, watching what the first lock found. Then select <em>Stable class row</em> and follow the same requests. The committed count changes only when a transaction commits.</p>
<LockFigure scene="coverage" number={1} />
<p>The unprotected run finished with two bookings for capacity one. Protect the interval from reading the facts used for the decision through committing its effect. Locking only while issuing the insert comes too late: the decision may already be stale.</p>

<h2 id="cover-every-change-that-could-invalidate-the-decision">Cover every change that could invalidate the decision</h2>
<p>The capacity decision depends on a class row and a changing set of reservations. A new reservation can invalidate it without changing any reservation row that already existed. If the set is empty, <code>SELECT … FROM reservations … FOR UPDATE</code> locks no reservation at all. In the native check, that empty locking read did not stop another session inserting a reservation.</p>
<p>More generally, a locking clause needs an engine contract. Do not infer that it reserves a condition such as “all rows matching class 21”, including future rows. PostgreSQL’s row locks protect selected rows; its serialisable predicate tracking serves a different purpose, explained in <a href="/review/relational-next/isolation/">Reading and testing isolation guarantees</a>.</p>
<p>Give every writer an explicit place in the protocol. We will have them acquire the class row before reading the facts for their change. For a move, that means both classes.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="The writers that share the class-capacity rule"><table><thead><tr><th>Writer</th><th>Acquire first</th><th>Decision under protection</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Reserve or import a booking</td><td>The destination class</td><td>Class exists and the new reservation fits; insert once.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Cancel</td><td>The booking’s class</td><td>Recheck that the booking still belongs there, then delete the existing row.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Move or reassign</td><td>Both classes, in the shared order</td><td>Recheck ownership and source, then check destination capacity and change the reservation.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Reduce capacity</td><td>The class</td><td>New capacity is at least its current reservation count.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Increase capacity</td><td>The class</td><td>Keep capacity nonnegative; this path cannot cause overselling, but still follows our common protocol.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Delete a class</td><td>The class</td><td>No reservations remain; the foreign key also refuses a referenced deletion.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Staff correction or bulk repair</td><td>Every affected class, in the shared order</td><td>The complete resulting state still fits. There is no administrative exception to the invariant.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>An ordinary report need not acquire these locks: it makes no capacity-changing decision. A pure cancellation or capacity increase cannot by itself increase count beyond capacity, so a narrower protocol could exempt it from <em>that</em> inequality. We keep both coordinated here to make moves, deletion and the booking’s location easier to reason about together. An exemption needs its own argument for every rule that operation touches.</p>

<h2 id="give-the-invariant-a-stable-coordination-row">Give the invariant a stable coordination row</h2>
<p>Class 21 exists even when it has no bookings. That makes its resource row a <strong>coordination row</strong>: all participants meet at the same stable identity before deciding. Lock the class first, then count its reservations in a separate command, then make the change, keeping the transaction open throughout.</p>
<Sql code={reserve} label="Reserve by counting ownership after acquiring the class row" />
<p>These are separate database commands. The application must check that the first returns exactly one row and stop if it does not. One returned request from the insert, followed by successful commit, accepts the booking. Zero inserted rows declines it. A constraint error fails the transaction and requires rollback. The request identity and owner are bound parameters in a real application; this worksheet spells out their values.</p>
<p>The second command checks both capacity and the count in its fresh Read Committed view. The previous holder may have changed either while we waited. Every relevant participant uses the same coordination row, so another conforming writer cannot invalidate that decision before our insert commits.</p>
<p>Creating the class is a separate operation: insert its unique identity and valid capacity, then commit before accepting bookings for it. A booking must never respond to a failed class lookup by silently creating another coordination mechanism. An absent row provides no row lock. The native absent-class insert failed its foreign key; that is reference enforcement, not a substitute capacity protocol.</p>
<p>Retain the row while bookings can refer to it. If the service allows physical deletion, its deletion path takes the same lock, verifies that no bookings remain and deletes in that transaction. A waiter must handle the class disappearing. Do not automatically recreate or reuse a deleted identity in a booking path; retire and restore resources through an explicit lifecycle.</p>
<p>There is one easy mistake in the order. Combining the locking read and the count into one SQL command does not give the count a new snapshot after the wait. In the recorded PostgreSQL run, the command below began while A held class 21 with R1 uncommitted. After A committed, B acquired the class but returned a count of zero.</p>
<Sql code={combined} label="A locking command can return a count from before its wait finished" />
<p>Step through Figure 2 with <em>Separate decision statement</em>, then switch to the combined version. Watch when the count is taken, rather than just when the lock arrives.</p>
<LockFigure scene="visibility" number={2} />
<p>The new command counted one in both native runs. The lock controls who can proceed; the statement boundary supplies the view our decision requires at this isolation level. Do not carry the recipe unchanged to Repeatable Read or another engine. Establish its read and abort contract first.</p>

<h2 id="choose-the-lock-and-keep-it-through-the-decision">Choose the lock and keep it through the decision</h2>
<p>PostgreSQL has several row lock modes. We use <code>FOR UPDATE</code> because two transactions requesting it on the same row conflict, and it also conflicts with updates, deletes and the key-share lock used by a foreign-key insertion. Only those interactions are needed to assess this protocol.</p>
<p>Figure 3 puts one competing operation beside the holder. Try the capacity update, deletion and reservation insert; then compare an ordinary count.</p>
<LockFigure scene="modes" number={3} />
<p>All three changing operations waited in the native checks. The ordinary count did not: it read the previous committed state. A row lock does not generally make versioned readers queue behind the holder.</p>
<p>A reservation insert’s foreign-key check also waited for our <code>FOR UPDATE</code> holder. That is useful to understand, but it does not make a bypassing insert safe. After the holder releases its lock, the foreign key checks only that the class exists. It can still insert a booking into a full class. Every reservation writer must acquire, count and decide explicitly.</p>
<p>The weaker <code>FOR NO KEY UPDATE</code> mode also conflicts with itself and could coordinate willing participants. It permits a key-share lock, so our native foreign-key insert completed while that weaker lock was held. Choosing a weaker mode changes those interactions; it does not remove the all-writers obligation. PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/explicit-locking.html#LOCKING-ROWS">row lock compatibility table</a> is the reference when changing the choice.</p>
<p>Keep the lock until commit or rollback. Autocommit would release a standalone locking query’s lock before the next decision command. A savepoint can also change the boundary: rolling back to a savepoint releases locks acquired after it. This recipe uses no savepoints. If you add one, reacquire any released protection and repeat the dependent reads before continuing.</p>
<p>Keep this interval short by preparing input and doing unrelated computation before <code>BEGIN</code>. The capacity read and reservation change stay inside. An HTTP call, a human confirmation or a slow report while holding the class makes every following booking wait; the lock does not make that external work transactional. The <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/">application branch</a> handles connection lifetimes and recoverable external work.</p>

<h2 id="acquire-several-resources-in-a-consistent-order">Acquire several resources in a consistent order</h2>
<p>Moving a reservation from 21 to 22 changes two classes. If A takes 21 then asks for 22, while B takes 22 then asks for 21, neither can finish. Each waits for a lock the other will release only after finishing. That cycle is a <strong>deadlock</strong>.</p>
<p>Play Figure 4 with opposite first locks. Then set <em>Both take 21, then 22</em>. B now waits before holding a class row, leaving A free to finish.</p>
<LockFigure scene="order" number={4} />
<p>Define a canonical order shared by every path: ascending distinct resource IDs. Acquire one ID at a time with explicit commands. A sorted result from a more elaborate query is not, by itself, evidence of the lock acquisition order we need. A move within one class acquires it once.</p>
<Sql code={move} label="Move R1 from 21 to 22, after taking both coordination rows in order" />
<p>A lookup made before acquisition can help find the expected source, but it cannot authorise the move. After acquiring both class rows, lock and recheck the reservation. If another move changed its source, roll back and reconsider using its current location. Do not acquire a newly discovered lower-numbered class after already holding a higher one. Apply the same rule to cancellation and staff tools.</p>
<p>The native reversed-order run produced one <code>40P01</code> deadlock error. A further statement in the victim’s transaction produced <code>25P02</code>, indicating the failed transaction. The survivor could continue. PostgreSQL chooses a victim; application logic must not depend on which one it chooses.</p>
<p>The canonical two-class run completed in sequence. It removes that cycle, not every possible deadlock. A foreign key, unique constraint, reservation row or another writer may introduce a different ordering dependency. Roll back a failed attempt and reconsider the complete operation, including its reads. <a href="/review/relational-next/request-recovery/">Recovering database requests safely</a> develops the client’s retry and outcome-handling policy.</p>

<h2 id="decide-what-waiting-and-skipping-mean-for-the-application">Decide what waiting and skipping mean for the application</h2>
<p>A popular class is now a contention point: its capacity decisions take turns. That is the protocol’s cost. A holder that pauses before commit makes later decisions wait even when their own SQL is quick.</p>
<p>Choose what the operation promises while another holder is active. PostgreSQL’s <code>lock_timeout</code> can bound an individual lock wait. <code>FOR UPDATE NOWAIT</code> refuses to wait for a conflicting row lock. Neither response means “the class is full”; the protected capacity decision has not happened.</p>
<p>Figure 5 compares a timed wait, refusal and skipping for the same locked candidate. Watch whether B gets an error or an empty result.</p>
<LockFigure scene="waiting" number={5} />
<p>The worksheet’s 150 ms lock timeout and its <code>NOWAIT</code> attempt both failed with <code>55P03</code>. Without a savepoint, these errors leave the transaction failed until rollback. A timeout limits that wait; it does not promise a complete request deadline, fair access or a successful next attempt.</p>
<p><code>SKIP LOCKED</code> changes the set returned. In the native check, asking for locked class 21 returned no rows even though an ordinary observer could see that it existed. Do not turn that into “class absent”, “no space” or “no work exists”. Skipping also gives no processing-order guarantee: a later candidate may pass one held by somebody else. PostgreSQL documents it for tasks such as multiple consumers claiming queue rows; leases and worker recovery belong in <a href="/review/relational-next/background-work/">Connecting transactions to background work and events</a>.</p>
<p>These row options do not remove every possible wait, including table locks needed by the command. Keep the operation’s failure response honest and its work bounded. General throughput and contention measurement continue in <a href="/review/relational-next/concurrent-load/">Improving performance under concurrent load</a>.</p>

<h2 id="check-the-protocol-with-two-sessions-and-an-all-writers-argument">Check the protocol with two sessions and an all-writers argument</h2>
<p>Use a disposable database and two independently connected clients, A and B. Run the schema once. Reset between cases after both clients have ended their transactions. The reset destroys this exercise’s reservations and resources.</p>
<Sql code={reset} label="Reset only the disposable locking exercise" />
<p>In both clients, start with <code>BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL READ COMMITTED</code>. Record the engine version and timeout settings. Confirm a wait using a separate observer’s <code>pg_stat_activity</code> entry with <code>wait_event_type = 'Lock'</code>, rather than treating a guessed sleep as proof.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Two-session worksheet with explicit pause points"><table><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Statement order and assertion</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Unprotected race</td><td>A and B each count zero for 21. A inserts <code>('R1',21,7)</code> and commits; B inserts <code>('R2',21,8)</code> and commits. Expect two reservations and <code>within_capacity = false</code>.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Protected wait</td><td>A locks class 21, inserts R1 and pauses. B’s ordinary count returns zero. B then requests the class lock; observe the wait. A commits. B acquires 21; its separate next count returns one. Decline R2 and roll back B. Expect one reservation and a true invariant.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Initially empty set</td><td>A locks matching reservation rows for 21, returning none. B inserts R1 and commits without waiting for A. End A. The empty set supplied no reservation lock.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Lock and count combined</td><td>A locks 21 and inserts uncommitted R1. B starts the displayed combined query and waits. A commits. Expect B’s combined result <code>21|0</code>; B’s separate next count returns one. Roll B back.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Absent resource</td><td>A’s locking lookup for 99 returns no row. A reservation insert referencing 99 fails <code>23503</code>; roll back. The booking protocol must stop at the missing lookup.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Reversed order</td><td>A locks 21; B locks 22. A requests 22 and waits. B requests 21. Expect one <code>40P01</code> victim; more SQL there returns <code>25P02</code>. Roll back both. Repeat with both taking 21 then 22; expect sequential completion.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The failed race is deliberate. For the protected run, check the final state as well as what the requests returned.</p>
<Sql code={inspect} label="Check each class’s committed reservation count against capacity" />
<p><a href={worksheetUrl} download="locking-rehearsal.py">Download the complete two-session worksheet</a> to run these schedules automatically with Python 3 and PostgreSQL’s command-line clients. It creates an unused database, records SQL, outputs, version and settings, asserts the outcomes and removes its database and sessions. Set <code>PGHOST</code>, <code>PGPORT</code> and <code>PGUSER</code> for a disposable server; <code>PG_BIN</code> optionally supplies its tools directory. Choose an unused name with <code>REL3_LOCK_DB</code>. Run <code>python3 locking-rehearsal.py &gt; result.json</code>. It requires database-creation rights and refuses an existing name.</p>
<p>The recorded run used PostgreSQL 18.6, Read Committed, a 12-second statement timeout and five-second lock timeout, with a session-local 150 ms timeout for that one test. The server’s deadlock timeout was one second. It also checked real capacity updates, deletions, foreign-key insertions, the weaker lock, <code>NOWAIT</code> and <code>SKIP LOCKED</code>. These are selected observations, not a workload measurement or a proof that all application writers comply.</p>
<p>Finish the argument in words. Every capacity-changing writer acquires the same stable class identity before its relevant reads. A multi-class change takes every required identity in the agreed order. The decision is made from a fresh Read Committed statement after acquisition. Each change preserves the rule when run alone, protection lasts through its outcome, and rollback leaves no partial result. The constraints still enforce identities and references.</p>
<p>A staff import that inserts without counting, a capacity reduction that skips its check, a move that trusts an earlier source, or a transaction that releases the lock before deciding breaks a premise. Selected runs cannot find every bypass. Review the actual writer inventory alongside the tests, and revisit both whenever a writer, query, isolation setting or engine changes.</p>

